evious house: not robbed / robbed\n        for x in nums:\n            skip, take = max(skip, take), skip + x\n        return max(skip, take)\n","approach":"**At each house the only decision is rob or skip, and the best outcome through house `i` depends only on the best outcomes through `i-1` and `i-2`.** Let `best(i)` be the maximum loot using houses `0..i`. Then `best(i) = max(best(i-1), best(i-2) + nums[i])`: either skip house i (keep best(i-1)) or rob it, which forces house i-1 to be skipped so we add to best(i-2).\n\nThe code keeps two numbers: `take`, the best total if the previous house was robbed, and `skip`, the best if it was not. Robbing the current house is only allowed on top of `skip`.\n\n**Why it works:** any legal plan restricted to the first i houses is still legal, so an optimal plan for 0..i is built from an optimal plan for a shorter prefix; there are no other interactions beyond adjacency.\n\n**Common mistakes:** assuming you must alternate houses (greedy on odd/even indices fails on [2,1,1,2]), and the O(n) array when O(1) space is expected.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Reconstruct which houses were robbed? (Store `best[]` as an array and walk backwards: if best[i] != best[i-1] then house i was taken and jump to i-2.) Houses in a circle? (House Robber II: run twice excluding the first or the last.) Houses arranged as a tree? (House Robber III: post-order DFS returning (rob, skip) per node.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["house-robber-ii","min-cost-climbing-stairs","delete-and-earn"],"number":124,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def rob(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}